\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{What zero incidence and scalar signs forget}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{The [signed endpoint equations](fdip-003B) retain information
that an incidence operator alone can lose. Take vertices #{\{0,1\}}
and one loop #{e} attached at #{0}. Its incidence map is zero over
every ring. Swapping the vertices, fixing #{e} and choosing sign #{+}
therefore intertwines incidence and preserves any weight on #{e}.
Nevertheless #{s(q(e))=0\ne1=p(s(e))}; the same obstruction holds
for sign #{-}, since both endpoints of the loop are #{0}.
This is a signed-incidence symmetry, but not a signed endpoint arrow.}

\p{A separate loss concerns composition. Let #{C_3=\{1,g,g^2\}}
fix a single loop and its vertex. Assign abstract signs
#{\varepsilon_1=+}, #{\varepsilon_g=\varepsilon_{g^2}=-}.
Every endpoint and zero-incidence equation holds separately. But the
action cocycle at #{g\cdot g=g^2} would require
##{\varepsilon_{g^2}=\varepsilon_g\varepsilon_g=+,}
contrary to the assigned #{-}. Thus these arrows do not define a
#{C_3}-action. Over a ring of characteristic two the scalar maps all
become the identity because #{-1=1}; even their representation equations
then conceal the failed abstract cocycle. Orientation data remain
subject to the abstract #{C_2} law.}

\p{By contrast, one edge #{e:0\to1} admits the vertex swap with
fixed edge label and sign #{-}. Its endpoints reverse as required,
and using this arrow twice gives sign #{+} and identity bijections.
For fields,
##{(dPf)(e)=f(0)-f(1)=-(df)(e)=(Jdf)(e).}
It is a valid signed symmetry. Parallel labels are still distinct:
an edge bijection may permute them, but never identifies two labels.
Each sign must satisfy its own endpoint rule and the composition law.}

\p{These calculations use the endpoint-difference construction of
\ref{fdip-003B}. They explain its extension of the arbitrary-orientation
convention in \citet{Section 2.1, pp. 4-5}{spielman2009effective}, and
the group-law requirement in
\citet{Definition 4.1, p. 57}{milne2025group}; neither source is being
asserted to contain these loop or characteristic-two examples.}
